      "en": "Answer: C (1793)\nTopic: Indian History | Subtopic: British Land Revenue Administration | Difficulty: Easy\nEnglish Explanation: The Permanent Settlement of Bengal (Permanent Settlement of Land Revenue) was introduced by Governor-General Lord Cornwallis on 22 March 1793. Developed in consultation with Sir John Shore, it recognized landlords (Zamindars) as absolute proprietors of land in return for paying a fixed perpetual revenue to the East India Company (fixed at 10/11th to the Company, 1/11th retained by the Zamindar).\nWhy Other Options Are Wrong: 1773 was the Regulating Act; 1784 was Pitt's India Act; 1813 was the Charter Act ending EIC trade monopoly.\nEnglish Exam Tip: Permanent Settlement = 1793 (Bengal, Bihar, Orissa) by Lord Cornwallis; Ryotwari = 1820 (Madras) by Thomas Munro; Mahalwari = 1822 (North-West) by Holt Mackenzie.",
