      "en": "Answer: B (Mahatma Gandhi and Dr. B.R. Ambedkar)\nTopic: Indian National Movement | Subtopic: Poona Pact & Depressed Classes | Difficulty: Easy\nEnglish Explanation: Following Ramsay MacDonald's announcement of the Communal Award granting separate electorates to the Depressed Classes, Mahatma Gandhi undertook a fast-unto-death in Yerwada Central Jail, Pune. Through the mediation of Madan Mohan Malaviya, Tej Bahadur Sapru, and C. Rajagopalachari, the historic Poona Pact was signed on 24 September 1932 between Dr. B.R. Ambedkar (on behalf of Depressed Classes) and Hindu leaders led by Madan Mohan Malaviya with Gandhi's direct assent. It replaced separate electorates with reserved seats in joint electorates, increasing reserved provincial seats from 71 to 147.\nWhy Other Options Are Wrong: Jinnah was not party to Poona Pact; Lord Willingdon was Viceroy who simply endorsed it; Nehru was in jail in northern India.\nEnglish Exam Tip: Poona Pact (24 September 1932): Signed between Dr. Ambedkar and caste Hindu leaders (approved by Gandhi in Yerwada jail); reserved provincial seats increased from 71 to 147.",
